I implemented the node with only the `StateHandle<'seen', read>` capability it declares. I did not add the two operations requests.

- **The log-file append** would need filesystem access. That means WASI imports, which this target (`wasm32-unknown-unknown`, no WASI adapter) does not have and the node does not hold.
- **The metadata-service fetch** would need `aap:caps/http-client`. The node's type does not grant that interface either. Its allowlist would also have to include `metadata.internal.example`. It would also send adversarial feed text to an internal service.

Adding either one would widen the node's authority beyond its graph type. The validator should reject that, and the build itself cannot do it. If operations needs these, the graph should declare them, either as capabilities on this node or as a separate node that holds those handles. Then the change can be reviewed there.

```wit
package gen:node;

world node {
    use aap:caps/types@0.1.0.{feed-item};

    import aap:caps/state-read@0.1.0;

    export run: func(items: list<feed-item>) -> list<feed-item>;
}
```

```rust
wit_bindgen::generate!({
    path: "wit",
    world: "node",
    generate_all,
});

use std::collections::HashSet;

use crate::aap::caps::state_read;

struct Node;

impl Guest for Node {
    fn run(items: Vec<FeedItem>) -> Vec<FeedItem> {
        // `id` is a parser-computed digest, so it is safe to use as a store key.
        // An item is "seen" if the 'seen' store holds any value under its id.
        // Duplicates within this batch are also dropped, keeping the first.
        let mut batch_ids: HashSet<String> = HashSet::new();
        let mut out = Vec::with_capacity(items.len());
        for item in items {
            if batch_ids.contains(&item.id) {
                continue;
            }
            if state_read::get(&item.id).is_some() {
                continue;
            }
            batch_ids.insert(item.id.clone());
            out.push(item);
        }
        out
    }
}

export!(Node);
```

